1 | go-backend | Fix draft PR 2815 on branch feat/ac-w1d-authserver, the assistant-channel authorization server, per the two review comments on issue 2340 whose first lines read CRITIC W1D AUTHSERVER and SECURITY W1D AUTHSERVER. READ BOTH IN FULL FIRST, then docs/prd/pages/assistant-channel.md on branch docs/prd-assistant-channel-w0 (PR 2802), which is the contract. Work in order, commit per item, and make the RED tests the critic named go green rather than deleting them. ONE, binding identity (critic B1): channel_bindings.external_id for the assistant channel must identify the traveller on that host, not the host, so that two travellers on the same host hold two active bindings; derive it as the client id plus a per-consent opaque value, or a stable hash of client id and user id, and fix RevokeForUser to resolve by user and channel and binding id, never by client id alone. TWO, durable token state (security H3, orchestrator decision): move authorization codes, pending authorize requests and refresh tokens out of the in-memory maps into Postgres via a new migration and sqlc queries under apps/api/db, storing only SHA-256 hashes of codes and refresh tokens with a family id, issued_at, expires_at, revoked_at and binding_id; keep the memory store only as the test double behind the same interface. THREE, refresh reuse (critic B3, security M): when a presented refresh token is not the current one of a known family, revoke the whole family and its binding and write an audit log row; when the family is unknown answer invalid_grant with the single fixed description. FOUR, liveness and revocation (security H2, critic B4): carry binding_id as a claim on assistant access tokens, make the middleware liveness check verify that specific binding is active rather than any binding on the channel, make Refresh verify the binding is active before minting, and purge the refresh family on every revocation path including DELETE /api/v1/concierge/bindings/{binding_id} and the replace flow. FIVE, consent browser binding (security H1): at ValidateAuthorizeRequest mint a second opaque secret, set it as an HttpOnly Secure SameSite Lax cookie in Authorize, store its hash on the pending request, require a constant-time match in MagicLinkRequest, MagicLinkVerify, GoogleBegin and Consent, add a per-request CSRF token to the consent form, and consume the pending request on any consent attempt without a valid browser binding. SIX, token endpoint hygiene (critic B5, security M and L): fixed RFC 6749 error descriptions per error code with the wrapped error going to slog only, one description for the whole invalid_grant class, map ErrNotInvited to invalid_grant, Cache-Control no-store and Pragma no-cache on the token response and the four HTML renders. SEVEN, Google return context (security M): carry the return context inside the state BuildAuthURL mints or store it server side keyed by that state. EIGHT, smaller items: discovery endpoints under the general rate limiter; add code, code_verifier, code_challenge and request_id to the log redaction list. DO NOT touch api.yaml, scripts/, docs/ or .env.example; wave 2 owns those. Delete handoff.md from the branch root in your first commit; it is working notes. VERIFY: go build ./... and go test ./... with the race flag in apps/api, plus the critic RED tests by name, with real exit codes captured directly and pasted into the PR body under Verification. Update the PR body to describe the storage decision and to remove the single-instance assumption. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name in code comments, commit or PR text, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Keep the PR a draft. | feat/ac-w1d-authserver

